  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Prove each of the following statements by breaking the proof into separate cases:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p12-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If two integers are both even or both odd, then their sum is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Even case: $m+n=2(a+b)$. Odd case: $m+n=2(a+b+1)$.\n\nThe sum is twice an integer in both cases.",
          "worked": "Case 1: both integers are even. Write $m=2a$ and $n=2b$, where $a,b\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n$$\\begin{align*}m+n&=2a+2b\\\\m+n&=2(a+b).\\end{align*}$$\nThus $m+n$ is even.\n\nCase 2: both integers are odd. Write $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b+1$, where $a,b\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n$$\\begin{align*}m+n&=2a+1+2b+1\\\\m+n&=2(a+b+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThus $m+n$ is even in both cases.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $n\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $5n^2+3n+7$ is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $n=2a$, the expression is $2(10a^2+3a+3)+1$; for $n=2a+1$, it is $2(10a^2+13a+7)+1$.\n\nBoth cases have the form twice an integer plus one.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $n$ is even. Write $n=2a$, where $a\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n$$\\begin{align*}5n^2+3n+7&=5(2a)^2+3(2a)+7\\\\5n^2+3n+7&=20a^2+6a+7\\\\5n^2+3n+7&=2(10a^2+3a+3)+1.\\end{align*}$$\nThis is odd.\n\nCase 2: $n$ is odd. Write $n=2a+1$, where $a\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n$$\\begin{align*}5n^2+3n+7&=5(2a+1)^2+3(2a+1)+7\\\\5n^2+3n+7&=20a^2+26a+15\\\\5n^2+3n+7&=2(10a^2+13a+7)+1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe expressions in parentheses are integers, so $5n^2+3n+7$ is odd in both cases.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If $n\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $n^2+3n+4$ is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $n=2a$, the expression is $2(2a^2+3a+2)$; for $n=2a+1$, it is $2(2a^2+5a+4)$.\n\nBoth cases are twice an integer.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $n$ is even. Write $n=2a$, where $a\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2+3n+4&=(2a)^2+3(2a)+4\\\\n^2+3n+4&=4a^2+6a+4\\\\n^2+3n+4&=2(2a^2+3a+2).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is even.\n\nCase 2: $n$ is odd. Write $n=2a+1$, where $a\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2+3n+4&=(2a+1)^2+3(2a+1)+4\\\\n^2+3n+4&=4a^2+10a+8\\\\n^2+3n+4&=2(2a^2+5a+4).\\end{align*}$$\nThe expressions in parentheses are integers, so $n^2+3n+4$ is even in both cases.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p12-q3-d-intro",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Suppose ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "n\\in\\mathbb N",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ". Prove that",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p12-q3-d-expression",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "1+(-1)^n(2n-1)",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p12-q3-d-conclusion",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "is a multiple of 4.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $n=2k$, the expression equals $4k$; for $n=2k+1$, it equals $-4k$.\n\nIt is a multiple of $4$ in both cases.",
          "worked": "Case 1: $n$ is even. Write $n=2k$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. Then $(-1)^n=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1+(-1)^n(2n-1)&=1+1\\bigl(2(2k)-1\\bigr)\\\\1+(-1)^n(2n-1)&=4k.\\end{align*}$$\nThis is a multiple of $4$.\n\nCase 2: $n$ is odd. Write $n=2k+1$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. Then $(-1)^n=-1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1+(-1)^n(2n-1)&=1-\\bigl(2(2k+1)-1\\bigr)\\\\1+(-1)^n(2n-1)&=1-(4k+1)\\\\1+(-1)^n(2n-1)&=-4k.\\end{align*}$$\nThis is also a multiple of $4$, proving the statement in both cases.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
